Review pass, rebased on the current 8.2 tip and squashed. What changed since the previous head:

**Test guarded the wrong environment variables.** `DoctrineDbalMysqlStoreTest::createMysqlConnection()` checked `MYSQL_DSN`, `MYSQL_USERNAME` and `MYSQL_PASSWORD`, assigned `$dsn`, `$user` and `$pass` without using them, then built the connection from `MYSQL_HOST`, which it never checked. `testDsnConstructor()` had the same defect. Running `--group integration` without `MYSQL_HOST` produced `Doctrine\DBAL\Exception\MalformedDsnException: Malformed database connection URL` on every test in the class instead of a skip. Both now go through one helper that guards on the three variables it actually uses, mirroring `DoctrineDbalPostgreSqlStoreTest`, which guards on `POSTGRES_HOST`.

**MariaDB connections were rejected.** The constructor checked `instanceof MySQLPlatform`, but DBAL 4 derives `MariaDBPlatform` from `AbstractMySQLPlatform`, not from `MySQLPlatform`, so `new DoctrineDbalMysqlStore($mariadbConnection)` threw "does not support the Doctrine\DBAL\Platforms\MariaDB1060Platform platform" while the class docblock claimed MariaDB support. The check is now `instanceof AbstractMySQLPlatform`, the same base `DoctrineDbalStore` already uses. `testSupportedPlatforms()` covers a MySQL and a MariaDB server version and fails on the old check.

**Timeout test could not run on MariaDB.** `testWaitAndSaveAfterConflictReleasesLockFromInternalStore()` used `SET SESSION max_execution_time`, which does not exist on MariaDB. The test now detects the platform and issues `max_statement_time` in seconds on MariaDB and `max_execution_time` in milliseconds on MySQL, rather than skipping the assertion, so the coverage holds on both servers. Only the MySQL path is exercised by CI, since the integration job has no MariaDB service.

**PHP CS Fixer.** Three of the four reported violations were real and are fixed: the double-quoted `SELECT IS_USED_LOCK(...)` string in `DoctrineDbalMysqlStore::exists()`, the spacing around the concatenation in the test, and the `InvalidArgumentException` import left behind in `StoreFactoryTest` when `testCreateStoreRejectsMysqlAdvisoryUrl()` was removed. `php-cs-fixer` is now clean over the whole component.

The fourth complaint, which asks to turn `"composer require %s"` into `"composer require "%s""` in `StoreFactory::assertClassExists()`, is a known false positive of that check and must not be applied. It targets pre-existing code this PR does not own, and the same suggestion was explicitly rejected on the earlier `MysqlStore` PR.

**Docblock.** Added two notes to the class: shared and read locks are unsupported because MySQL has no `pg_advisory_lock_shared`, so `Lock::acquireRead()` silently degrades to an exclusive lock; and the connection should be dedicated to locking, because advisory locks are session scoped and a `RELEASE_ALL_LOCKS()`, an `EntityManager::close()`, a `wait_timeout` expiration or a transparent DBAL reconnection releases every lock held without the store noticing.

**`MysqlStore` aligned.** The PDO store implemented only `PersistingStoreInterface` while the new DBAL store is blocking, which left the MySQL pair asymmetric against the PostgreSQL pair, where both classes are blocking. Its `save()` body moved into a private `saveWithTimeout(Key $key, int $timeout)`, `save()` passes `0`, a new `waitAndSave()` passes `-1`, and the class now implements `BlockingStoreInterface`. The two distinct placeholders stay, since a repeated named placeholder fails under PDO with `ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES=false`, and the timeout is bound explicitly with `\PDO::PARAM_INT` so the cached statement stays reusable across both timeouts. `MysqlStoreTest` now uses `BlockingStoreTestTrait`, the same shared suite `PostgreSqlStoreTest` uses, so the new capability is covered by the common tests rather than a hand-written duplicate. `MysqlStore` is unreleased, so there is no BC concern. No shared or read lock support was added to either store, and the same two docblock notes were applied to both, plus a typo fix in the `delete()` comment of each.

The CHANGELOG is unchanged: it names `mysql+advisory://` only, and the `mysql2` alias is deliberately not advertised.

Verification: `./phpunit src/Symfony/Component/Lock` gives 479 tests, 185 skipped, with no failure attributable to this PR (the local Redis, `CombinedStore`, `PdoStore` and `DoctrineDbalStore` noise reproduces identically on the merge base). The MySQL-backed tests skip locally for lack of a server and a `pdo_mysql` extension; the integration job covers them. `php-cs-fixer` and PHPStan level 5 are clean on the changed files.
